# SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0

@prefix dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix fnd: <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/foundation#> .
@prefix ldg: <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/ledger#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix qnt: <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/quantification#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix voc: <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/vocabulary#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/ledger> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/ledger/0.20.0> ;
    owl:imports <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/foundation/0.20.0>, <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/quantification/0.20.0>, <https://www.nebularis.org/neuro-semantic/lattice/vocabulary/0.20.0> ;
    dct:description """Ledger holds amounts that can be consumed and restored, such as a limit, commitment, seat quota, carbon budget, and so on. These are represented by an \"account\", configured by how much it holds when fresh, what unit it's held in, and the rule for arbitrating several claims on it at once. Accounts form a tree by \"part of and not in addition to\", so a single draw or credit can affect several accounts at once, along one stated path.

In order to keep a history of changes to an account, weigh several claims on it together, and let different implementations agree on a shared record, each draw or credit is recorded as a fact of its own: a claim against the account that leaves its stated amount untouched. A balance is always derived by folding an operation over those facts. Conservation then holds by construction, since no balance can disagree with the facts it is derived from. Recording a claim and applying it also become separate acts, so claims made together can be held unresolved and decided together by a stated merge rule.

Separate facts require two forms of ordering. A strict before and after ensures a balance can be read 'as of' a given point, and a mark of which facts belong together ensures they can be decided as one. Wall-clock time can supply neither (since clocks on different machines do not agree closely enough to order two claims), so an evaluator assigns the order deterministically, such that any two implementations given the same inputs will assign the same order.

Each fact carries a whole number placing it among the other facts on its account, called its position, and a second number for ordering facts that share a position, called its micro-position. The fact itself, containing one draw or credit with its account, owner, amount, direction and position, is referred to as a posting.

This one central law then, governs balances: each is a stated amount less what has been drawn, plus what has been restored, ensuring a balance is cheap to compute, but provable across properties checkable as a closed, mechanical law.

Since the effect of a posting is exactly the account plus every ancestor reached by following the ldg:partOf property upward, one fixed edge at a time, the set of accounts a draw touches is always fully enumerable (by walking one chain and checking each link), with no ambiguity about which balances a draw changes.

Avoiding storing a balance also ensures that a balance agrees with its posting history by construction, with no path by which someone might set a balance to a value the postings don't justify.

When disparate evaluations draw on the same account at the same position (being logically simultaneous, not \"whoever runs first\"), the model follows a fixed path, 'propose -> merge -> settle'.

- Propose - each evaluation states what it requires (e.g., a requested value)
- Merge - the account's own merge rule (priority order / proportionality / refusal) looks at the whole batch together and decides every grant in one pass, producing one merge record
- Settle - each evaluation then posts whatever it was actually granted

Because the merge step is a pure function of the complete set of proposals, the result can't depend on what order the evaluations happened to run in. No evaluation's outcome therefore, depends on the order in which the others ran, so they may run in parallel. Given exact arithmetic in the account's unit, the account's stated rounding and residual rules, and ties broken by an order defined on the data rather than on arrival, two conforming implementations reach the same grants in any execution order, and the same bytes once their records are written in canonical form. Conformance cases check this.

Ledger accounts do not hold observed values such as prices or temperatures, which are intensive and carry no such law.""" ;
    dct:creator <https://github.com/nebularis> .

dct:creator a owl:AnnotationProperty .

dct:description a owl:AnnotationProperty .

ldg:AccountDefinition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "An amount consumed and restored, as the terms state it." ;
    fnd:utility "Declare one of these for each amount a contract states as a limit, commitment, quota or budget: a software licence's seat pool, a facility's commitment, a grant's budget, an aggregate or a sublimit. State its amount, its unit of account, the kinds of owner it admits and its merge rule always; state a parent, a reset, a key dimension, purposes it counts against, a credit ceiling or a conversion method only where the terms need them." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:admitsOwnerKind ; owl:minCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:conversionMethod ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:creditCeiling ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:keyDimension ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:mergeRule ; owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:partOf ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:resetRecurrence ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:residualAllocation ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:roundingMethod ; owl:maxCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:statedAmount ; owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ], [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ldg:unitOfAccount ; owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] .

ldg:admitsOwnerKind a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "The kinds of owner this account admits." ;
    fnd:utility "Names, by class, who may own this account: an instrument, an authority, an ensemble such as a programme or a vehicle, or any other fnd:PersistentIdentity. State one value for each kind the terms allow." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Class .

ldg:conversionMethod a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "How a draw in another value space than the account's is converted." ;
    fnd:utility "States the standing conversion arrangement for a draw made in a different value space from the account's own (a sterling commitment drawn in euros). The conversion context a specific draw used, which may differ by date, is recorded with that draw." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range qnt:ConversionContext .

ldg:countsPurpose a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "A purpose which, when a draw names it, also debits this account outside its path." ;
    fnd:utility "Lets an account outside the drawn path still be debited by a draw that names a purpose this account counts, so a cross-cutting sub-aggregate (a flood aggregate beside a peril-neutral tree) still erodes with the rest. State one value per purpose this account counts." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept .

ldg:creditCeiling a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "The most a credit restores this account to." ;
    fnd:utility "States the ceiling a credit may never restore the balance above. Defaults to the stated amount where unset. State a ceiling above the stated amount for a reinstatement (the aggregate's own stated amount stays the per-layer limit, and the ceiling lets one reinstatement credit raise the balance to twice it, without a second account)." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range qnt:Quantity .

ldg:keyDimension a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "The dimension that derives one account per key value." ;
    fnd:utility "States that this definition's amount applies once per key value (a site, a borrower), each key's account derived independently on the first draw that names it. Point at a skos:ConceptScheme naming the key values, or at a qnt:ValueSpace where the key is itself a quantity. Leave unset for an account with no key." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range skos:ConceptScheme .

ldg:mergeRule a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "What happens when several draws at one position together exceed availability." ;
    fnd:utility "Selects the arbitration rule for this account: ldg:InPriorityOrder, ldg:Proportional (also state ldg:roundingMethod and ldg:residualAllocation), or ldg:Refuse (the whole merge is refused, with no grant, where proposals together overdraw the account)." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept .

ldg:partOf a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "\"Part of and not in addition to\": the parent edge of the account tree." ;
    fnd:utility "States that this account's balance is drawn from its parent's too, along the same path. An account outside another's subtree is in addition to it, never drawn through it, so state no ldg:partOf for a limit that sits beside another rather than inside it. The edge is stated, never inferred, and may not cycle." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range ldg:AccountDefinition .

ldg:resetRecurrence a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "When the account returns to its stated amount." ;
    fnd:utility "States a recurrence (an annual seat licence, a quarterly support-hours bundle) at whose boundary the account resets. Leave unset for an account that never resets." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range qnt:Recurrence .

ldg:residualAllocation a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "How the rounding residual is allocated so a proportional merge's grants still sum to availability." ;
    fnd:utility "Required wherever ldg:mergeRule is ldg:Proportional. Rounding every share independently can leave the sum short of, or over, availability by a unit or two; state how that residual is allocated: ldg:LargestRemainder (ties broken by the total order of the proposals) or ldg:ToFirstInOrder." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept .

ldg:roundingMethod a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "How a proportional merge's exact shares are rounded to the account's unit." ;
    fnd:utility "Required wherever ldg:mergeRule is ldg:Proportional. An exact proportional share need not be a whole unit of account, so state how it rounds: ldg:HalfEven, ldg:HalfUp or ldg:TowardZero." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept .

ldg:statedAmount a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "The amount when fresh." ;
    fnd:utility "States an account definition's amount before any draw, in its unit of account. Point at a qnt:Quantity on the same space named by ldg:unitOfAccount." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range qnt:Quantity .

ldg:unitOfAccount a owl:FunctionalProperty, owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "The value space every posting on this account is in." ;
    fnd:utility "Names the value space an account's amounts are stated and posted in. The space must be extensive (qnt:additivity qnt:Extensive) with qnt:Sum and qnt:Difference capabilities, since Ledger holds only amounts whose parts add to the whole (§1)." ;
    rdfs:domain ldg:AccountDefinition ;
    rdfs:range qnt:ValueSpace .
